1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,680
The Personal Protective Equipment, PPE, worn by actors in this video, was selected based on a

2
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:11,360
risk assessment of the chemicals used in the experiment and conditions simulated in the activity.

3
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:14,960
In scenes where the presenter did not wear PPE while in the lab,

4
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:18,000
all chemicals were removed to eliminate chemical hazards.

5
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:23,680
In this video, you'll learn general methods and strategies for minimizing risk when using

6
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:28,800
chemicals and lab equipment. Minimizing risk is part of a larger process called RAMP.

7
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:34,720
RAMP stands for Recognize Hazards, Assess Risks, Minimize Risks, and Prepare for Emergencies.

8
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:39,440
For this video, we'll assume you've already completed the first two steps of RAMP,

9
00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:46,960
Recognize Hazards, and Assess Risks. The first step in minimizing risk is to think about how

10
00:00:46,960 --> 00:00:51,920
you could be exposed to chemicals in the lab. There are four ways chemicals can enter your body.

11
00:00:51,920 --> 00:00:57,440
The first is inhalation, the most common route of exposure. Some chemicals are used in gas form,

12
00:00:57,520 --> 00:01:02,640
which makes it easy to accidentally inhale them. But many liquid chemicals also produce a vapor.

13
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:07,680
Some solids even give off fumes. Anytime you smell a chemical, you are inhaling the gas,

14
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:13,600
but you might not smell all gases. Some have no odor, while others may be too dilute to smell,

15
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:17,840
but still cause harm. The second most common route of exposure is direct contact with the

16
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:22,240
skin or eyes. You can consider these separate routes of exposure because the tissues of the

17
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:27,600
eye are extremely sensitive to damage. Two less common routes of exposure are ingesting a chemical

18
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:31,760
and injecting a chemical through the skin. Injection can happen if you cut yourself on

19
00:01:31,760 --> 00:01:37,760
broken glassware that is contaminated with a chemical. Ingestion isn't very common since

20
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:41,680
tasting, drinking, or eating lab chemicals or food is strictly forbidden in the lab.

21
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:49,620
Once we've identified these potential routes of exposure, minimizing risks is the next step.

22
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:53,700
A common way to think about reducing risk is the hierarchy of controls.

23
00:01:53,700 --> 00:01:58,260
This inverted pyramid shows the most effective strategies at the top and the least effective or

24
00:01:58,260 --> 00:02:03,380
less reliable at the bottom. At the top of the pyramid is elimination, which is the best way to

25
00:02:03,380 --> 00:02:08,900
minimize risk. If there's no hazard, there's obviously no risk. If we can't completely

26
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:14,020
eliminate the hazard, we may be able to reduce the scale. In other words, perform a smaller version

27
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:19,220
of the experiment. That way, if something goes wrong, the effect will be less. A smaller scale

28
00:02:19,220 --> 00:02:24,420
means it's cheaper too. Substitution can have the same effect as elimination. For example,

29
00:02:24,420 --> 00:02:29,140
if a solvent is flammable, perhaps it can be replaced with another solvent that is less flammable.

30
00:02:29,140 --> 00:02:33,060
Options one and two at the top of the pyramid have more to do with designing experiments.

31
00:02:33,060 --> 00:02:37,460
Your instructors have probably considered these already. So let's focus on the lower three

32
00:02:37,460 --> 00:02:41,380
options, which are steps you can take while in the lab to minimize exposure.

33
00:02:41,380 --> 00:02:45,380
Remember, strategies that reduce the likelihood of exposure also reduce the risk.

34
00:02:46,100 --> 00:02:52,180
In the lab, engineering controls usually refers to using a fume hood. The main purpose of a hood

35
00:02:52,180 --> 00:02:57,140
is to remove gases and vapors. This lowers the chance of inhalation, as well as fire or

36
00:02:57,140 --> 00:03:02,740
explosions. It's important to note that a hood is most effective only when used correctly. Using

37
00:03:02,740 --> 00:03:06,980
any equipment the wrong way could lead to a false sense of security. Check to make sure

38
00:03:06,980 --> 00:03:11,380
the hood is on by holding a tissue at the face of the hood. Always look for a test sticker that

39
00:03:11,380 --> 00:03:16,100
tells you the equipment is being maintained. Also, is the equipment positioned at least six

40
00:03:16,100 --> 00:03:21,700
inches away from the sash? This is important so the hood can effectively capture gases and vapors.

41
00:03:21,700 --> 00:03:25,380
Are there any unnecessary containers or equipment that could block airflow?

42
00:03:25,380 --> 00:03:30,340
Are there stored chemicals that could catch fire? Is the sash down? Hoods aren't designed

43
00:03:30,340 --> 00:03:34,900
to contain a fiery disaster, but they'll help lessen their impact, particularly when the sash

44
00:03:34,900 --> 00:03:40,020
is fully closed. Administrative controls are a wide range of behaviors that can help minimize

45
00:03:40,020 --> 00:03:45,460
exposure and lower risk in the lab. Your college or university will have its own set of lab rules.

46
00:03:46,260 --> 00:03:50,420
All of these were developed with risk minimization in mind, and you should know and follow these

47
00:03:50,420 --> 00:03:55,620
rules in your labs. Prepare for each experiment by reading the safety precautions provided in the

48
00:03:55,620 --> 00:04:00,180
directions. Follow experimental procedures as written since they were developed with safety

49
00:04:00,180 --> 00:04:06,180
in mind. Finally, let's talk about personal protective equipment, PPE, and lab dress, the

50
00:04:06,180 --> 00:04:10,900
last line of defense in preventing exposure. Proper eye protection is required in all labs

51
00:04:10,900 --> 00:04:15,780
where chemicals are used, and there's a possibility of chemical splash. For most situations, you'll

52
00:04:15,780 --> 00:04:21,380
want to wear indirectly vented splash goggles. Many institutions require these in all chemistry

53
00:04:21,380 --> 00:04:26,340
labs at all times. Some allow safety glasses. You need to know and follow what your institution

54
00:04:26,340 --> 00:04:32,260
requires. What about dress? It's really quite simple. To minimize exposure, cover as much scan

55
00:04:32,260 --> 00:04:38,260
as possible. It's smarter to wear long sleeves rather than short sleeves. Natural fiber materials

56
00:04:38,260 --> 00:04:43,140
are best since synthetic fabrics burn more easily. Many institutions will also require

57
00:04:43,140 --> 00:04:47,780
use of a lab coat. Just like eye protection, you need to know the local rules for clothing

58
00:04:47,780 --> 00:04:53,300
and lab coats. Skin exposure is always possible when handling chemicals, and gloves are the best

59
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:58,100
way to prevent this. Lab gloves should be considered one-time use disposable gloves.

60
00:04:58,740 --> 00:05:02,580
You should select a material that is resistant to whatever chemical you're using.

61
00:05:02,580 --> 00:05:06,820
There are easy-to-find resources online that describe how various glove materials protect

62
00:05:06,820 --> 00:05:11,780
against chemicals. In lab class, the best glove material has usually been determined for you.

63
00:05:11,780 --> 00:05:16,180
Your instructor will advise you on the specific rules for using gloves at your institution.

64
00:05:16,180 --> 00:05:21,220
Remember that chemicals can be transferred from your gloves to any item you touch—a calculator,

65
00:05:21,220 --> 00:05:26,260
computer, or cell phone. Chemicals can also be transferred to your pen and into the

66
00:05:26,260 --> 00:05:30,260
body if you place it in your mouth. This kind of chemical transfer is why food and drinks

67
00:05:30,260 --> 00:05:35,460
aren't allowed in the lab. Finally, remember to always remove your gloves before leaving lab

68
00:05:35,460 --> 00:05:40,980
and to wash your hands at the end of class. To minimize exposure, it's important to remove

69
00:05:40,980 --> 00:05:46,020
gloves correctly so chemicals don't contact your skin. To start, pinch the first glove to remove

70
00:05:46,020 --> 00:05:50,180
close to the wrist and start peeling it off while turning it inside out. Crumble the removed

71
00:05:50,180 --> 00:05:55,140
glove in the palm of the gloved hand. Now use your ungloved hand to peel off the second glove

72
00:05:55,140 --> 00:06:00,180
and enclose the crumpled one. Discard these used gloves as directed by your instructor.

73
00:06:02,860 --> 00:06:07,820
When thinking about minimizing risk, what level is acceptable? In your labs, the risks have likely

74
00:06:07,820 --> 00:06:12,220
been reduced to a reasonably low level by your instructor, but the risks might be medium when

75
00:06:12,220 --> 00:06:17,580
using particularly hazardous chemicals where no good substitute exists. In research labs,

76
00:06:17,580 --> 00:06:22,140
the question of when to accept higher risk is more complicated. You may face this question

77
00:06:22,140 --> 00:06:26,060
someday. Staying safe in the lab requires constant vigilance about hazards and the

78
00:06:26,140 --> 00:06:30,860
steps to minimize risk. Thinking about routes of exposure, when to use a hood,

79
00:06:30,860 --> 00:06:35,180
and what PPE to wear will help keep you safe. To learn more about the other steps in RAMP,

80
00:06:35,180 --> 00:06:39,340
be sure to check out the other five videos in this series. You can find them by clicking here,

81
00:06:39,900 --> 00:06:44,540
checking out the links in the description, or visiting acs.org slash safety videos.
